{"ts":"2026-06-03T01:30:55Z","session":"c9c139dd-bb56-444e-872a-061412982a9a","event":"prompt","text":"what exactly is expected for section 6 # 6. Testing AI Performance\n\nIn this section, first write down the most likely AI failure cases, then run a set of tests against your prototype to see which of those failures can be detected today.\n\n## Part A: Anticipate Plausible Failures\n\nIdentify at least **5 realistic ways** your AI coworker or workflow could fail.\n\n| Failure Case                                 | Why It Might Happen             | Business Consequence       | How the User Would Notice            | How Your Process Handles It            |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |\n| AI uses the wrong document                   | Retrieval finds a weak match    | Wrong recommendation       | Source shown does not match the case | Human rejects output and reruns search |\n| AI output sounds confident but is incomplete | Missing data                    | Poor decision              | Required fields are blank            | System blocks final approval           |\n| User gives vague input                       | The starting request is unclear | AI produces generic output | Output lacks specific details        | Ask user for missing information       |\n\nFocus on **practical failures**, not abstract risks.\n\n### Examples\n\n* Missing data\n* Conflicting sources\n* Wrong document retrieved\n* Incorrect classification\n* Bad recommendation\n* Output is too generic\n* User skips review\n* Tool or API fails\n* AI produces a result that looks polished but is not supported by evidence\n* The process is faster but less accurate\n\n---\n\n## Part B: Develop Tests\n\nAnyone can list failures on paper. The harder skill—and the one your future employer will actually pay for—is checking whether the AI behaves correctly across many cases before a real user ever sees a bad answer.\n\nUse **DeepEval**, a free, open-source testing tool that works with Pyt"}
